<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kangju</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 450 CE (approx.) · capital Beitian</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A long-lived nomad kingdom of the middle Syr Darya, a rival of the Wusun and Dayuan that dominated the steppe north of Sogdiana for six centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kangju first appears in the account of the Han envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>, who passed through around 128 BCE and reported a nomadic state north-west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dayuan">Dayuan</a> with some 80,000 to 90,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. Its people were Iranian-speaking pastoralists on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and the Talas and Chu valleys, part of the wider <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> world.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>According to the Han histories, the king had his seat at the city of Beitian and a separate winter residence, with five lesser kings beneath him. Kangju controlled routes between the steppe, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samarkand">Sogdiana</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dayuan">Kingdom of Dayuan</a>, and at times held Sogdian towns as dependencies. In 36 BCE it sheltered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhizhi">Zhizhi</a>, whom a Han army killed there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kangju is linked by archaeologists to the Kaunchi and Otrar-Karatau cultures. It weakened under pressure from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kidarite-kingdom">Kidarite Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalites</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran</a>, and by the 5th century its name passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samarkand">Sogdiana</a> itself, 'Kang' being the later Chinese name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kangju.jpg" alt="Kangju" width="320" height="274"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lessman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territory_of_the_Kangju_in_200_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beitian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iranian steppe religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eastern Iranian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>K'ang-chu, Kangju kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=kangju" aria-label="Kangju on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6262 470 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kangju lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6582 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Kazakhstan. <a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=kangju">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

